+      <article id="journal-article" class="journal-article">
+        <p>Every exceptional cup of coffee begins long before the first crack in our roastery drum—it starts on high-altitude mountain slopes where volcanic soil, cool night air, and patient cultivation converge. At The Daily Grind, our bean sourcing process is built exclusively on direct-trade partnerships with smallholder farmers and producer cooperatives across Ethiopia, Colombia, Guatemala, and Sumatra. By bypassing multi-layered commodity brokers, we work face-to-face with the growers who nurture each coffee cherry from seedling to harvest, paying at least 40% above Fair Trade minimums so farming families can reinvest in sustainable agriculture and community infrastructure.</p>
+        <p>During harvest season, our green coffee buyers travel directly to origin to cup hundreds of micro-lots alongside local agronomists. We select only 100% specialty-grade Arabica cherries that have been selectively hand-picked at peak ripeness, ensuring uniform natural sugars and vibrant acidity. Whether a lot undergoes washed processing in crystal-clear mountain spring water, honey processing on raised African drying beds, or traditional wet-hulling, we track moisture content and water activity at every stage. Only lots scoring 86 points or higher on the Specialty Coffee Association scale earn a place on our seasonal menu.</p>
+        <p>Sustainability and traceability guide how our beans travel from farm to roastery. All green coffee is sealed in hermetic GrainPro liners inside biodegradable jute bags to lock in terroir and freshness during ocean transit. Once the bags arrive at our local roastery, we conduct sample roasts and blind sensory cuppings to design a custom roast profile for each harvest lot—highlighting delicate jasmine and bergamot notes in our washed Ethiopians or rich dark cacao and toasted hazelnut undertones in our Central American lots. Every batch is roasted to order in small quantities so the coffee in your cup honors the craft of everyone who touched it along the way.</p>
+      </article>
